# Outpost Chess (Vercel edition)

Pass-and-play and live online chess. This version is built to deploy on Vercel:
- Static pages (`landingpage.html`, `mainpage.html`, `chessEngine.js`) are served directly.
- `api/ws.js` is a Vercel Function that accepts WebSocket connections (Vercel's native WS
  support, public beta) and validates every move server-side using the shared chess engine.
- Game/room state lives in **Redis**, not in memory — necessary because a Vercel Function's
  instances don't share memory, so two players' sockets could land on different instances.
  Redis Pub/Sub relays moves between instances.

## 1. Create a free Redis database (Upstash)

1. Go to https://upstash.com, sign up free, create a Redis database (any region close to
   where you'll deploy is fine).
2. On the database page, copy the **ioredis / Redis URL** connection string — it looks like
   `rediss://default:<password>@<host>:<port>`.

## 2. Set the environment variable on Vercel

In your Vercel project: **Settings → Environment Variables** →
add `REDIS_URL` = the connection string from step 1 → save for Production (and Preview/Dev
if you want previews to work too).

## 3. Deploy

Push this repo to GitHub, then in Vercel: **New Project → Import** your repo → Deploy.
No build command or output directory is needed — it's a zero-config static + Functions project.

You'll get a free domain like `outpost-chess.vercel.app`. A custom domain (e.g.
`outpostchess.com`) can be added under **Settings → Domains** once you own one from any registrar.

## Local development

```bash
npm install -g vercel   # if you don't already have the CLI
npm install
vercel link             # connect this folder to your Vercel project
vercel env pull .env.local   # pulls REDIS_URL down locally
vercel dev               # serves everything on http://localhost:3000, api/ included
```

## Known trade-offs (worth knowing, not bugs)

- **5-minute WebSocket cap**: Vercel force-closes any WebSocket connection after 300 seconds,
  even mid-game, on every plan. The client automatically reconnects using the saved room
  token, so this shows briefly as "Reconnecting…" rather than ending the game — but it will
  happen roughly every 5 minutes in a long game.
- **Match history and lobby data live in Redis**, not a JSON file, so they survive redeploys
  (unlike a plain filesystem on serverless).
- **No distributed locking on moves**: two near-simultaneous writes to the same room are
  rare in a turn-based game (only one player can legally move at a time) but not physically
  impossible if a client double-sends. Fine for a portfolio project; a production app would
  add optimistic locking (e.g. a version number checked on save).
- Threefold repetition is not implemented as a draw condition (50-move rule and insufficient
  material are).